<h2>The case and the custody</h2>

<p>The Jal Jeevan Mission tender case arises from FIR No. 245/2024, registered on 30 October 2024 by the Anti-Corruption Bureau, Jaipur, alleging irregularities in the award of tenders under the flagship rural water supply programme. Mahesh Chandra Joshi, aged 72, had been in custody in this case since 7 May 2026 — after already spending over seven months in custody in a connected money-laundering case, in which the Supreme Court itself granted him bail on 3 December 2025. Mukesh Pathak had been in custody since 18 February 2026, and already held bail in both the CBI case and the money-laundering case arising from the same controversy. The Rajasthan High Court nonetheless rejected both bail applications on 13 August 2026.</p>

<h2>What weighed with the Supreme Court</h2>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Probe complete</h3><p>Investigation had concluded and chargesheets were filed; no custodial interrogation remained to be done.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Documentary case</h3><p>The prosecution rests on documents and electronic records already in the agency's hands — material the accused cannot tamper with from liberty any more than from jail.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Distant trial</h3><p>Twenty-two accused, about 124 witnesses and nearly 20,000 pages of documents, with charges yet to be framed: a trial nowhere in sight.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Parity and age</h3><p>Bail already granted in the connected prosecutions, and the advanced age of one appellant, reinforced the conclusion.</p></div>
</div>

<p>Against that matrix, the Bench held that the seriousness of the allegations could not by itself justify continued detention, observing that further pre-trial incarceration of the appellants was neither warranted nor necessary, and that prolonged pre-trial detention cannot be permitted to degenerate into punishment without trial. The appeals were allowed, the High Court's order was set aside, and both appellants were directed to be released on bail.</p>

<h2>The doctrinal line this judgment extends</h2>

<p>The judgment sits in a now-familiar line of Supreme Court authority — from the classic formulation that bail is the rule and jail the exception, through the recent decisions emphasising Article 21 where chargesheets are filed and trials are stalled by sheer volume. The operative logic is practical rather than sentimental. Custody pending trial is preventive, not punitive: it is justified only by flight risk, tampering risk, or the needs of investigation. Once the investigation is over and the evidence is documentary, each of those justifications falls away, and what remains — detention because the charge sounds grave — is constitutionally impermissible. Courts hearing bail applications in economic-offence and corruption matters in Delhi's district courts and the High Court invoke the same framework daily, and this judgment adds a recent, directly applicable precedent.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>FIR · Oct 2024</h3><p>ACB Jaipur registers FIR No. 245/2024 over JJM tender irregularities.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Arrests · 2026</h3><p>Pathak in custody from 18 February 2026; Joshi from 7 May 2026.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>HC refusal · Aug 2026</h3><p>Rajasthan High Court rejects both bail applications on 13 August 2026.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>SC bail · Oct 2026</h3><p>Supreme Court allows the appeals and grants bail on conditions.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What the Court did not decide</h2>

<p>The Bench was careful to keep the merits untouched. Arguments on successive FIRs covering the same transaction, on the want of prior approval under Section 17A of the Prevention of Corruption Act, 1988, and on the legality of the arrests were all noticed but left open for the trial and for appropriate proceedings. The judgment therefore cannot be read as any finding on the strength of the prosecution case — only as a finding that liberty pending that determination had been withheld for too long, for reasons the law does not recognise.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, regular bail in non-bailable offences continues to be governed by the framework formerly found in Sections 437 and 439 CrPC, now Sections 480 and 483 BNSS. The principles in this judgment apply unchanged to BNSS-era bail applications.</p></div>

<p>For practitioners, the judgment is most useful for its checklist quality: completed investigation, documentary evidence, filed chargesheet, unframed charges, large witness lists and long custody together make a compelling bail matrix, and a refusal on gravity alone is vulnerable in appeal. For accused persons and their families, it is a reminder that the Supreme Court continues to police the line between detention as a safeguard and detention as a sanction.</p>
